1
00:00:02,100 --> 00:00:05,060
Your argument is lacking in all scientific merit.

2
00:00:05,060 --> 00:00:10,390
It is well established superman cleans his uniform by flying into Earth's yellow sun,

3
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,360
which incinerates any contaminate matter

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:16,920
and leaves the invulnerable kryptonian fabric unharmed and daisy fresh.

5
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:20,220
What if he gets something Kryptonian on it?

6
00:00:20,290 --> 00:00:21,320
Like what?

7
00:00:21,390 --> 00:00:24,260
I don't know. Kryptonian mustard.

8
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:26,890
I think we can safely assume that all Kryptonian condiments

9
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:30,760
were destroyed when the planet Krypton exploded.

10
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:32,260
Or it turned into mustard Kryptonite,

11
00:00:32,320 --> 00:00:36,690
the only way to destroy a rogue Kryptonian hotdog threatening Earth.

12
00:00:36,760 --> 00:00:38,160
Raj, please,

13
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:43,960
let's stay serious here. Superman's body is Kryptonian, therefore his sweat is Kryptonian.

14
00:00:44,020 --> 00:00:46,620
Yeah, what about Kryptonian pit stains?

15
00:00:46,690 --> 00:00:48,560
Superman doesn't sweat on Earth.

16
00:00:48,620 --> 00:00:52,060
Okay, he's invited for dinner in the Bottle City of Kandor.

17
00:00:52,120 --> 00:00:55,060
He miniaturizes himself, enters the city where he loses his superpowers. Now,

18
00:00:55,120 --> 00:00:58,590
before dinner, his host says, "Who's up for a little Kryptonian tetherball?"

19
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:01,180
Superman says "Sure," works up a sweat, comes back to Earth,

20
00:01:01,180 --> 00:01:04,720
his uniform now stained with indestructible Kryptonian perspiration.

21
00:01:07,260 --> 00:01:09,860
Boo ya.

22
00:01:11,590 --> 00:01:13,190
Superman would have taken his uniform

23
00:01:13,260 --> 00:01:15,720
to a Kandorian dry cleaner before he left the Bottle.

24
00:01:17,260 --> 00:01:18,890
"Kandorian dry cl..." I give up.

25
00:01:18,960 --> 00:01:21,260
You can't have a rational argument with this man.

26
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:26,660
isn't that the guy who won the MacArthur genius grant last year?

27
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,590
No, not all at once.

28
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:31,090
Then how?

29
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:32,360
Leonard.

30
00:01:34,090 --> 00:01:35,260
Now, Raj.

31
00:01:37,420 --> 00:01:38,590
Now, Sheldon.

32
00:01:41,060 --> 00:01:42,490
I didn't get a good look. Can I go again?

33
00:01:42,560 --> 00:01:43,990
No.

34
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:46,020
It's David Underhill. So what?

35
00:01:46,090 --> 00:01:47,160
So what?

36
00:01:47,220 --> 00:01:49,360
His observation of high-energy positrons

37
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,060
has provided the first conclusive evidence

38
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:53,090
for the existence of galactic dark matter.

39
00:01:53,160 --> 00:01:54,290
I have two words for you.

40
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:55,690
The first is "big," the other's "whoop."

41
00:01:55,760 --> 00:01:57,820
It is a big whoop.

42
00:01:57,890 --> 00:01:59,260
It made almost all the work

43
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:01,060
you've done since you've been here completely useless.

44
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:02,220
Did not. Did, too.

45
00:02:02,290 --> 00:02:03,320
Did...

46
00:02:03,390 --> 00:02:04,590
Okay, maybe  some of it, but...

47
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:08,120
Look, the guy was just in the right place,

48
00:02:08,190 --> 00:02:09,760
at the right time with the right paradigm-shifting

49
00:02:09,820 --> 00:02:13,320
reinterpretation of the universe. He got lucky.

50
00:02:13,390 --> 00:02:14,660
In more ways than one.

51
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:17,990
He's a very handsome man.

52
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:20,460
Doesn't do anything for me. If I was gonna go that way,

53
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:23,760
I'm more of a Zac Ephron kinda guy.

54
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:28,090
Oh, yeah, like you have a shot with Zac Ephron.

55
00:02:29,460 --> 00:02:30,390
Excuse me...

56
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:32,190
Are you Leonard Hofstadter?

57
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:33,620
Uh... yeah.

58
00:02:33,690 --> 00:02:34,690
I'm David Underhill.

59
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:38,020
Uh... y-yeah.

60
00:02:38,090 --> 00:02:39,460
Yeah, Dr. Gablehauser said if I wanted

61
00:02:39,520 --> 00:02:41,090
to set something up in the photomultiplier lab,

62
00:02:41,160 --> 00:02:42,920
that you'd be able to give me a hand?

63
00:02:42,990 --> 00:02:44,860
You want to work with me? Well,

64
00:02:44,920 --> 00:02:46,090
if you have a little time, yeah.

65
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,590
Wow.... Y-yeah, sure.

66
00:02:48,660 --> 00:02:50,560
Yeah, no problem. Uh...

67
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:52,020
Here's my home number.

68
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:53,020
Here's my cell.

69
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:55,020
Here's my office.

70
00:02:55,090 --> 00:02:58,220
Here's my parents' number up in New Jersey.

71
00:02:58,290 --> 00:03:00,090
They always know how to reach me. So... Okay.

72
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:03,090
Congratulations on the MacArthur Grant, by the way. Big fan.

73
00:03:03,160 --> 00:03:05,220
Thanks. I'll call you.

74
00:03:05,290 --> 00:03:06,990
Okay.

75
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:08,760
Bye-bye!

76
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:13,720
What are you looking at?

77
00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:15,220
You've never seen a hypocrite before?

78
00:03:42,020 --> 00:03:43,660
Trailing badly, Wolowitz needs a strike

79
00:03:43,720 --> 00:03:46,120
if he has any hopes of catching up with Sheldon Cooper,

80
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:50,340
who is dominating in the ninth frame with a career-best 68.

81
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:02,120
Hey, guys!

82
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:03,260
That doesn't count.

83
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:04,760
Do over! Do over!

84
00:04:04,820 --> 00:04:07,260
There are no do-overs in Wii bowling.

85
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:11,590
There are always do-overs when my people play sports.

86
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:15,460
Where were you that's more important than Wii bowling night?

87
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:16,460
Actually, I was...

88
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:20,260
It's a rhetorical question. There is nothing more important than Wii bowling night.

89
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:22,490
Come on, it's just a video game.

90
00:04:22,560 --> 00:04:23,520
And we suck at it.

91
00:04:24,760 --> 00:04:27,860
Nice motivational speech from the team captain.

92
00:04:28,790 --> 00:04:29,820
Where were you?

93
00:04:29,890 --> 00:04:31,460
I was working with Dave Underhill.

94
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:35,720
Ooh, "Dave." Sounds like Leonard's got a new BFF.

95
00:04:35,790 --> 00:04:37,990
Actually, he's pretty cool.

96
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:40,320
I mean, not only is he a brilliant scientist,

97
00:04:40,390 --> 00:04:42,720
but it turns out he's a Black Diamond skier.

98
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:45,990
He collects vintage motorcycles. He plays in a rock band.

99
00:04:46,060 --> 00:04:48,860
So? We're in a rock band.

100
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:49,860
No...

101
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:53,290
We play Rock Band on our X-Box.

102
00:04:53,360 --> 00:04:57,390
Nice motivational speech from our lead guitarist.

103
00:04:57,460 --> 00:04:58,460
He's funny, too.

104
00:04:58,520 --> 00:05:00,620
He does this hysterical impersonation

105
00:05:00,690 --> 00:05:02,920
of Stephen Hawking having phone sex.

106
00:05:04,020 --> 00:05:06,860
What are you wearing?

107
00:05:08,060 --> 00:05:09,590
That's not... He does it better.

108
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,290
Anyway, he said he was gonna take me to the gym tomorrow,

109
00:05:12,360 --> 00:05:13,990
so I'm gonna go practice my situps.

110
00:05:14,990 --> 00:05:18,990
Whoa. Humongous man crush, dude.

111
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:22,720
Yeah. It's officially a bro-mance.

112
00:05:22,790 --> 00:05:25,990
Hey, Sheldon, are you and Leonard putting up a Christmas tree?

113
00:05:26,060 --> 00:05:27,620
No, because we don't celebrate

114
00:05:27,690 --> 00:05:30,290
the ancient pagan festival of Saturnalia.

115
00:05:31,690 --> 00:05:32,860
Saturnalia?

116
00:05:32,920 --> 00:05:34,190
Gather round, kids,

117
00:05:34,260 --> 00:05:38,590
it's time for Sheldon's beloved Christmas special.

118
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:40,990
In the pre-Christian era,

119
00:05:41,060 --> 00:05:43,520
as the winter solstice approached and the plants died,

120
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,990
pagans brought evergreen boughs into their homes

121
00:05:46,060 --> 00:05:48,020
as an act of sympathetic magic, intended

122
00:05:48,090 --> 00:05:50,920
to guard the life essences of the plants until spring.

123
00:05:50,990 --> 00:05:52,660
This custom was later appropriated

124
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:54,660
by Northern Europeans and eventually it becomes

125
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:56,720
the so-called Christmas tree.

126
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:02,490
And that, Charlie Brown, is what boredom is all about.

127
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:05,160
Okay, well, thank you

128
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:06,760
for that, but I got you and Leonard

129
00:06:06,820 --> 00:06:08,320
a few silly neighbor gifts, so I'll just

130
00:06:08,390 --> 00:06:09,320
put them under my tree.

131
00:06:09,390 --> 00:06:10,190
Wait!

132
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,220
You bought me a present?

133
00:06:13,360 --> 00:06:15,390
Why would you do such a thing?

134
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:18,090
I don't know. 'Cause it's Christmas?

135
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,590
Oh, Penny. I know you think you're being generous,

136
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:23,490
but the foundation of gift-giving is reciprocity.

137
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,160
You haven't given me a gift.

138
00:06:25,220 --> 00:06:27,690
You've given me an obligation.

139
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:31,960
Don't feel bad, Penny, it's a classic rookie mistake.

140
00:06:32,020 --> 00:06:33,360
My first Hanukkah with Sheldon,

141
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,120
he yelled at me for eight nights.

142
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:39,590
Now, hey, it's okay.You don't have to get me anything in return.

143
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:41,460
Of course I do. The essence of the custom

144
00:06:41,520 --> 00:06:43,920
is that I now have to go out and purchase for you

145
00:06:43,990 --> 00:06:45,420
a gift of commensurate value

146
00:06:45,490 --> 00:06:47,660
and representing the same perceived level of friendship

147
00:06:47,720 --> 00:06:50,860
as that represented by the gift you've given me.

148
00:06:50,920 --> 00:06:54,160
It's no wonder suicide rates skyrocket this time of year.

149
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:55,890
Okay, you know what? Forget it.

150
00:06:55,960 --> 00:06:57,160
I'm not giving you a present.

151
00:06:57,220 --> 00:06:59,120
No, it's too late. I see it. That elf sticker

152
00:06:59,190 --> 00:07:00,290
says, "To Sheldon."

153
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:03,120
The die has been cast.

154
00:07:08,390 --> 00:07:11,720
I know. It's funny when it's not happening to us.

155
00:07:12,790 --> 00:07:16,390
Sheldon, I am very, very sorry.

156
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:18,090
No. No, I brought this on myself

157
00:07:18,160 --> 00:07:21,620
by being such an endearing and important part of your life.

158
00:07:22,790 --> 00:07:25,190
I'm going to need a ride to the mall.

159
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:28,490
It's happening to us.

160
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:33,820
Are you gonna make it?

161
00:07:33,890 --> 00:07:35,020
Yeah, I guess.

162
00:07:35,090 --> 00:07:36,220
All right.

163
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:39,620
Thanks for letting me try out your motorcycle.

164
00:07:39,690 --> 00:07:42,160
No problem.

165
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:45,520
I had no idea it was so heavy.

166
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:48,320
The thing just fell right over on me, didn't it?

167
00:07:48,390 --> 00:07:51,020
Yeah. Lucky for you it wasn't moving.

168
00:07:51,090 --> 00:07:53,990
-Oh, hey, Leonard. Ooh, are you okay? -Oh, yeah.

169
00:07:54,060 --> 00:07:56,490
It's just a little motorcycle accident.

170
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,360
My God, how fast were you going?

171
00:07:58,420 --> 00:08:00,920
I don't know. It's all such a blur.

172
00:08:02,520 --> 00:08:04,490
Good one. He couldn't even get it started.

173
00:08:05,390 --> 00:08:06,990
Hi. Dave. Hi.

174
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:09,120
Penny. So it's your motorcycle?

175
00:08:09,990 --> 00:08:10,920
Is it okay?

176
00:08:10,990 --> 00:08:11,920
It's fine.

177
00:08:11,990 --> 00:08:13,590
Lucky for the bike

178
00:08:13,660 --> 00:08:15,820
it landed on my leg.

179
00:08:15,890 --> 00:08:18,120
You mind giving me a hand with speed racer here?

180
00:08:18,190 --> 00:08:19,360
Oh, yeah. Yeah, sure.

181
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:21,960
So, um, Dave, how do you know Leonard?

182
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:23,160
I'm a physicist.

183
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:26,490
No, you're not.

184
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:28,290
Why is that so surprising?

185
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:30,820
Uh, well, it's just that the physicists I know

186
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:34,490
are indoorsy and pale.

187
00:08:34,560 --> 00:08:35,820
I'm not indoorsy.

188
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:37,490
I just wear the appropriate sun block

189
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:39,720
because I don't take melanoma lightly.

190
00:08:39,790 --> 00:08:40,960
So, are you

191
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:42,720
and Leonard working on an experiment together?

192
00:08:42,790 --> 00:08:43,960
Yeah, actually we are.

193
00:08:44,020 --> 00:08:45,490
Yeah, we're examining the radiation levels

194
00:08:45,560 --> 00:08:46,520
of photomultiplier tubes

195
00:08:46,590 --> 00:08:47,990
for a new dark matter detector.

196
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:49,090
Uh, sweetie, sweetie,

197
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:51,420
Dave was talking.

198
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:53,660
You know, I love science.

199
00:08:53,720 --> 00:08:55,990
-Since when? -Since always.

200
00:08:56,060 --> 00:08:58,320
Call me a geek, but I am just nuts

201
00:08:58,390 --> 00:09:00,790
for the whole subatomic- particle thing.

202
00:09:00,860 --> 00:09:03,520
The last thing I would ever call you is a geek.

203
00:09:03,590 --> 00:09:05,460
Ha. Well, that's what I am--

204
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:06,790
queen of the nerds.

205
00:09:06,860 --> 00:09:08,820
Well, if you like,

206
00:09:08,890 --> 00:09:10,790
I could show you the lab we're working in.

207
00:09:10,860 --> 00:09:13,260
We've got some cool toys, you know-- lasers and stuff.

208
00:09:13,320 --> 00:09:16,720
You know, I have always wanted to see a big science lab.

209
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:17,720
Since when?

210
00:09:17,790 --> 00:09:19,560
Since always.

211
00:09:19,620 --> 00:09:21,260
Leonard, are you okay here? Uh...

212
00:09:21,320 --> 00:09:23,490
-Yeah. I guess. -How 'bout we go see it now?

213
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:25,420
Maybe afterwards we take the bike up the coast,

214
00:09:25,490 --> 00:09:26,860
We grab a little bite to eat...

215
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:28,560
Um... Yeah. Yeah, that sounds great.

216
00:09:28,620 --> 00:09:29,820
Let me just get my jacket.

217
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:37,620
Boy, she'll do, huh?

218
00:09:37,690 --> 00:09:40,160
Yeah, if you like that type.

219
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,260
So, you and her...?

220
00:09:42,320 --> 00:09:43,320
No, just neighbors.

221
00:09:43,390 --> 00:09:44,590
Really.

222
00:09:44,660 --> 00:09:46,660
I don't know how you live next door to that

223
00:09:46,720 --> 00:09:48,760
without doing something about it.

224
00:09:49,890 --> 00:09:51,960
Actually...

225
00:09:52,020 --> 00:09:53,660
science is my lady.

226
00:09:56,220 --> 00:09:57,160
Okay. Let's go.

227
00:09:57,220 --> 00:09:58,160
All right.

228
00:09:58,220 --> 00:09:59,520
See you tomorrow, Leonard.

229
00:09:59,590 --> 00:10:01,520
See ya. 'Bye, Penny.

230
00:10:01,590 --> 00:10:03,160
Have fun.

231
00:10:07,720 --> 00:10:08,920
Yes?

232
00:10:16,560 --> 00:10:18,560
Did you forget your key?

233
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:33,190
I don't see anything in here a woman would want.

234
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:35,320
You're kidding.

235
00:10:35,390 --> 00:10:38,590
You've got lotions and bath oils and soaps.

236
00:10:38,660 --> 00:10:41,720
that's the estrogen hat trick.

237
00:10:41,790 --> 00:10:45,190
What it is is a cacophonous assault

238
00:10:45,260 --> 00:10:47,920
of eucalyptus, bayberry, cinnamon and vanilla.

239
00:10:47,990 --> 00:10:52,320
It's as if my head were trapped in the pajamas of a sultan.

240
00:10:52,390 --> 00:10:54,420
Sheldon, if you don't like this stuff,

241
00:10:54,490 --> 00:10:57,590
let's just go next door and build her a bear.

242
00:10:57,660 --> 00:11:01,360
I told you before, bears are terrifying.

243
00:11:01,420 --> 00:11:03,260
Come on,

244
00:11:03,320 --> 00:11:05,190
bath stuff. It's perfect!

245
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:08,090
You got a scented candle, a cleansing buff,

246
00:11:08,160 --> 00:11:10,790
spearmint and green tea scented bath oil, promotes relaxation.

247
00:11:10,860 --> 00:11:12,560
That presupposes Penny is tense.

248
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:13,890
She knows you. She's tense.

249
00:11:13,960 --> 00:11:15,590
We all are. Buy a basket!

250
00:11:15,660 --> 00:11:17,090
Excuse me, we're ready.

251
00:11:17,160 --> 00:11:18,620
No, we're not.

252
00:11:18,690 --> 00:11:20,590
Let's say for a moment that I accept

253
00:11:20,660 --> 00:11:22,390
the bath item gift hypothesis,

254
00:11:22,460 --> 00:11:24,460
I now lay the following conundrum at your feet:

255
00:11:24,520 --> 00:11:26,890
Which size?

256
00:11:26,960 --> 00:11:28,820
This one. Let's go.

257
00:11:28,890 --> 00:11:30,420
You put no thought into that.

258
00:11:30,490 --> 00:11:32,860
I'm sorry. Uh... this one.

259
00:11:32,920 --> 00:11:34,760
Let's go!

260
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:37,760
I have insufficient data to proceed.

261
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:39,160
Excuse me, miss?

262
00:11:39,220 --> 00:11:40,390
Yes?

263
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:43,160
If I were to give you this gift basket,

264
00:11:43,220 --> 00:11:45,690
based on that action alone and no other data,

265
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:50,460
infer and describe the hypothetical relationship that exists between us.

266
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:53,290
Excuse me?

267
00:11:53,360 --> 00:11:55,420
Here.

268
00:11:55,490 --> 00:11:57,660
Now, are we friends?

269
00:11:57,720 --> 00:12:00,490
Colleagues? Lovers?

270
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:02,620
Are you my grandmother?

271
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:06,320
I don't understand what you're talking about

272
00:12:06,390 --> 00:12:08,460
and you're making me a little uncomfortable.

273
00:12:10,120 --> 00:12:11,260
See? Sounds just like you and Penny.

274
00:12:11,320 --> 00:12:12,490
We'll take it.

275
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,160
Hey, Leonard. Come, join us.

276
00:12:19,520 --> 00:12:20,560
Oh, hey, Dave.

277
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:22,820
And Penny, what a surprise.

278
00:12:22,890 --> 00:12:24,190
Hey, Leonard.

279
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:25,420
Dave was just showing me around the university.

280
00:12:25,490 --> 00:12:27,920
You know, this place is unbelievable!

281
00:12:27,990 --> 00:12:31,760
Yeah, I know. I've been offering to show you around for a year and a half.

282
00:12:31,820 --> 00:12:33,590
You always said you had yoga.

283
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:35,320
I never said that.

284
00:12:35,390 --> 00:12:37,260
Maybe I heard you wrong.

285
00:12:37,320 --> 00:12:39,760
A lot of words sound like "yoga."

286
00:12:39,820 --> 00:12:42,090
This is an amazing woman, Leonard.

287
00:12:42,160 --> 00:12:44,290
She has a curious and agile mind,

288
00:12:44,360 --> 00:12:47,360
not to mention being curious and agile in other respects.

289
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:49,360
Oh, shut up!

290
00:12:49,420 --> 00:12:52,020
Yes, please shut up.

291
00:12:52,090 --> 00:12:54,590
So, um, Dave,

292
00:12:54,660 --> 00:12:56,960
don't you think you and I should get back to the lab?

293
00:12:57,020 --> 00:12:59,360
You know, that dark matter isn't going to detect itself.

294
00:13:03,390 --> 00:13:05,490
Actually, I was thinking about taking the afternoon off

295
00:13:05,560 --> 00:13:07,420
so I could work on another experiment with Penny.

296
00:13:07,490 --> 00:13:10,260
Really? We're going to do an experiment?

297
00:13:10,320 --> 00:13:12,290
Uh-huh. We're going to explore the effects of tequila shots

298
00:13:12,360 --> 00:13:14,790
on a gorgeous 22-year-old woman.

299
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:16,120
It's not an experiment!

300
00:13:16,190 --> 00:13:18,120
You saw what happened last night.

301
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:21,520
You ready to go? Yeah.

302
00:13:21,590 --> 00:13:22,760
Oh, can I drive the motorcycle?

303
00:13:22,820 --> 00:13:24,020
Yeah, why not?

304
00:13:24,090 --> 00:13:26,190
You can't do any worse than Leonard.

305
00:13:26,260 --> 00:13:28,560
That's funny.

306
00:13:30,690 --> 00:13:32,520
By the way, my leg is killing me.

307
00:13:32,590 --> 00:13:34,890
Thanks for asking.

308
00:13:55,900 --> 00:13:59,200
Mmm, great news, Leonard.

309
00:13:59,260 --> 00:14:01,460
I've solved my Penny gift dilemma.

310
00:14:01,530 --> 00:14:03,760
Yippee.

311
00:14:03,830 --> 00:14:06,830
You see, the danger was that I might under or over-reciprocate,

312
00:14:06,900 --> 00:14:08,830
but I have devised a foolproof plan.

313
00:14:08,900 --> 00:14:11,760
See, I will open her gift to me first

314
00:14:11,830 --> 00:14:13,200
and then excuse myself,

315
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:16,030
feigning digestive distress.

316
00:14:16,100 --> 00:14:18,030
Then I'll look up the price of her gift online,

317
00:14:18,100 --> 00:14:21,730
choose the basket closest to that value, give it to her

318
00:14:21,800 --> 00:14:25,530
and then I'll return the others for a full refund.

319
00:14:25,600 --> 00:14:27,930
Brilliant.

320
00:14:28,000 --> 00:14:30,460
It is, isn't it?

321
00:14:30,530 --> 00:14:33,100
Is it okay if I hide them in your room?

322
00:14:33,160 --> 00:14:36,100
The smell makes me nauseated.

323
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:37,500
Do whatever you want.

324
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:39,430
Thank you, that's very gracious.

325
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:42,230
Gentlemen.

326
00:14:46,100 --> 00:14:48,730
Why couldn't you have just done what Leonard did

327
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:51,730
and get Penny a new boyfriend?

328
00:14:53,800 --> 00:14:56,460
My leg is killing me.

329
00:14:56,530 --> 00:14:58,830
Thanks for asking.

330
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,700
Okay, I have just one question for you.

331
00:15:11,760 --> 00:15:14,500
While I am perfectly happy with the way things are between us,

332
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:15,900
you said that you didn't want

333
00:15:15,960 --> 00:15:17,960
to go out with me because I was too smart for you!

334
00:15:18,030 --> 00:15:20,260
Well, news flash, lady

335
00:15:20,330 --> 00:15:22,730
David Underhill is ten times smarter than me!

336
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:25,030
You'd have to drive a railroad spike into his brain

337
00:15:25,100 --> 00:15:27,360
for me to beat him at checkers!

338
00:15:27,430 --> 00:15:28,630
Next to him,

339
00:15:28,700 --> 00:15:30,760
I'm like one of those sign-language gorillas

340
00:15:30,830 --> 00:15:33,030
who knows how to ask for grapes!

341
00:15:34,600 --> 00:15:36,060
So, my question is...

342
00:15:36,130 --> 00:15:39,100
What's up with that?!

343
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:42,530
Why are you yelling at me?

344
00:15:42,600 --> 00:15:44,630
Sorry, I'm sorry, I'm sorry!

345
00:15:44,700 --> 00:15:47,260
Never mind, we're cool.

346
00:15:47,330 --> 00:15:48,730
Dave is not smarter than you.

347
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,030
He's an idiot.

348
00:15:51,100 --> 00:15:54,360
Really? Why would you say that?

349
00:15:54,430 --> 00:15:56,830
Because a smart guy takes the nude photos of his wife

350
00:15:56,900 --> 00:16:02,100
off his cell phone before he tries to take nude photos of his girlfriend.

351
00:16:03,130 --> 00:16:04,660
He tried to take nude photos of you?

352
00:16:04,730 --> 00:16:08,600
That's what you took from that?!

353
00:16:09,830 --> 00:16:12,560
The guy is married!

354
00:16:12,630 --> 00:16:13,960
Oh, yeah.

355
00:16:14,030 --> 00:16:17,660
I'm so-- Oh, that's terrible.

356
00:16:17,730 --> 00:16:19,360
And you, if you are so okay

357
00:16:19,430 --> 00:16:20,530
with the way things are between us,

358
00:16:20,600 --> 00:16:21,900
why are you so jealous?

359
00:16:21,960 --> 00:16:23,230
Well, uh...

360
00:16:25,160 --> 00:16:28,560
The important thing is he's married and that's terrible!

361
00:16:30,630 --> 00:16:33,130
Nice save, genius.

362
00:16:33,230 --> 00:16:34,600
Eggnog?

363
00:16:34,660 --> 00:16:36,930
Lactose.

364
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,960
It's just rum.

365
00:16:39,030 --> 00:16:42,400
It stopped being eggnog like half an hour ago.

366
00:16:45,860 --> 00:16:49,100
Smooth.

367
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:52,500
Smoother than you.

368
00:16:52,560 --> 00:16:56,160
Come on, it's Christmas, just give me this one.

369
00:16:57,230 --> 00:17:01,060
Okay, Merry Christmas.

370
00:17:05,360 --> 00:17:07,360
By the way, my leg is killing me.

371
00:17:07,430 --> 00:17:10,400
Thanks for asking.

372
00:17:17,360 --> 00:17:19,000
- Merry Christmas. - Merry Christmas.

373
00:17:19,060 --> 00:17:20,100
How's your leg?

374
00:17:20,160 --> 00:17:21,430
Very good, thanks for asking. Come on in.

375
00:17:23,430 --> 00:17:27,400
Ah, good, Penny, you're here to exchange gifts.

376
00:17:30,700 --> 00:17:31,960
You'll be pleased to know

377
00:17:32,030 --> 00:17:35,330
I'm prepared for whatever you have to offer.

378
00:17:35,400 --> 00:17:36,930
Okay, here.

379
00:17:39,960 --> 00:17:44,330
I should note I'm having some digestive distress,

380
00:17:46,130 --> 00:17:50,930
so, if I excuse myself abruptly, don't be alarmed.

381
00:17:57,460 --> 00:18:01,400
Oh, a napkin.

382
00:18:01,460 --> 00:18:04,330
Turn it over.

383
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:12,360
"To Sheldon,

384
00:18:12,430 --> 00:18:15,800
"live long and prosper.

385
00:18:15,860 --> 00:18:19,100
Leonard Nimoy."

386
00:18:20,800 --> 00:18:23,060
Yeah, he came into the restaurant.

387
00:18:23,130 --> 00:18:25,200
Sorry the napkin's dirty. He wiped his mouth with it.

388
00:18:30,800 --> 00:18:34,800
I possess the DNA of Leonard Nimoy?!

389
00:18:38,160 --> 00:18:41,300
Well...

390
00:18:42,400 --> 00:18:43,960
Yeah, yeah. I guess.

391
00:18:44,030 --> 00:18:45,930
But look, he signed it.

392
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,860
Do you realize what this means?!

393
00:18:50,160 --> 00:18:52,700
All I need is a healthy ovum

394
00:18:52,760 --> 00:18:57,030
and I can grow my own Leonard Nimoy!

395
00:19:00,100 --> 00:19:04,230
Okay, all I'm giving you is the napkin, Sheldon.

396
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:08,400
Be right back.

397
00:19:10,760 --> 00:19:12,760
Here. Open it.

398
00:19:15,130 --> 00:19:20,230
Oh, a gift cerficate for motorcycle lessons.

399
00:19:22,130 --> 00:19:24,130
Very thoughtful.

400
00:19:24,200 --> 00:19:25,160
Yeah, and I checked.

401
00:19:25,230 --> 00:19:26,600
Not letting the bike fall on you

402
00:19:26,660 --> 00:19:29,630
while standing still is lesson one.

403
00:19:29,700 --> 00:19:33,100
Oh, then, I think you'll appreciate what I got you.

404
00:19:33,160 --> 00:19:35,160
Okay.

405
00:19:37,730 --> 00:19:41,700
"101 Totally Cool Science Experiments for Kids."

406
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:49,460
You know, 'cause you're so into science.

407
00:19:59,760 --> 00:20:02,600
Sheldon! What did you do?!

408
00:20:02,660 --> 00:20:04,760
I know!

409
00:20:04,830 --> 00:20:07,630
It's not enough, is it?

410
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:11,800
Here.

411
00:20:24,300 --> 00:20:28,360
Leonard, look! Sheldon's hugging me.

412
00:20:28,430 --> 00:20:31,830
It's a Saturnalia miracle.

